the majority of licensed medical  physicians practicing medicine the us  today are MDS  also known as medical doctors however  there is also an increasing number of  practicing physicians known as Dios  which means doctors of osteopathic  medicine both are equally licensed to  practice medicine in all 50 states I  want to make one point very clear before  we start one degree is not better than  the other in fact they have so many  similarities you probably take an hour  to cover them this video is gonna focus  only on the differences between an MD  and a deal the most evident difference  between the two is that do is practice  something called om t which is  osteopathic manipulative therapy instead  of just telling you I'd much rather show  you  come on osteopathic manipulation is a  hands-on therapeutic tool whereby  manipulating the body's own tissues can  restore and maintain optimal function  and it's a low risk conservative measure  that can be used in place of medication  and surgery and it treats all types of  ailments everything from low back pain  to something more complex like  congestive heart failure take a deep  breath in deep breath out how's that  feel really good in osteopathic medical  school there's a heavy emphasis place on  the holistic medical approach now what  does that mean it means an extra focus  on the body's own ability to heal itself  a focus on prevention and a focus on the  whole patient instead of just a disease  process just because there's a heavier  emphasis place on this holistic model in  osteopathic school it doesn't mean that  MDS don't practice with this model as  well there are plenty of MDS who treat  with the whole patient philosophy in  mind those first two differences were  just differences in medical school  training now let's move on to residency  which is arguably the most important  part of medical training in order for a  residency program to be considered  legitimate needs to be authorized by an  Accreditation Council and currently  there are two accreditation counselors  one for MDS and one for do z/os can  train under either Accreditation Council  however md's can only train under the MD  banner come 2020 those two accreditation  councils will merge and allow residents  to train under unify  council further strengthening in my  point that there are becoming less and  less differences between the two degrees  now how about practicing internationally  well here MDS have a distinct advantage  as they're able to practice medicine in  more countries than do currently about  50 countries allow full medical  licensing for us trained EOS  with more countries allowing restricted  practicing as well this restriction  stems from the confusion created by  European trained osteopaths  who aren't medical doctors but are only  trained in manipulation of the  musculoskeletal system however the AOA  has been making great strides in  educating foreign governments about us  trained osteopathic doctors now what  about the applicants to get into an MD  or do program is extremely competitive  with the number of applicants and score  requirements rising each year it's  important to note that do schools do  accept a non-traditional Medical School  applicant those who are going into  medicine as their second career or  perhaps later in life or don't have a  scientific background also the GPA MCAT  combo required for admission is slightly  lower for do schools but that gap is  closing each year interestingly because  there are less do schools despite the  lower requirements it's still more  competitive to get into a deal school a  couple more differences there's a 9 to 1  ratio of m.d. studios out there with  about a hundred thousand do is in active  practice and to a higher percentage of  dio's go into primary care specialties  like family medicine ob/gyn or  pediatrics me personally I chose the do  route because I'm a fan of treating the  patient as a whole and the prevention  aspect plus it never 